=== layout: multiple-choice

@question

[\*\*\*]{color=red} After telophase I of meiosis, after the first cell division, the chromosomal makeup of each daughter cell is \_\_\_\_\_.

- A) Diploid; one chromatid per chromosome
- B) Diploid; two sister chromatids per chromosome
- C) Haploid; one chromatid per chromosome
- D) Haploid; two sister chromatids per chromosome

@answer

[Answer\: D]{color=red}

=== layout: one-panel

# Matter of Clarification

@body

- [*Haploid*]{underline=true} – a single set of chromosomes
- [*Diploid*]{underline=true} – two complete sets of chromosomes, [one from each parent]{color=red}
- Later when we discuss polyploids (week 11 or so), we will distinguish\:
  - [*monoploid*]{underline=true} – basic set of chromosomes that is multiplied in a polyploid
  - [*haploid*]{underline=true} – chromosomes present in a gamete, independent of total chromosome number – usually half the chromosome number
- For diploids\:
  - monoploid = haploid


